            <title xml:id="title_id66c92bd6" xml:lang="de">Johann Adolph Scheibens, marckgräfl. Brandenburg-Culmbachischen Capellmeisters, Lebens-Lauff, von ihm selbst entworffen</title>
            <title type="text_source" xml:id="title_18b9a41a" xml:lang="de">Grundlage einer Ehren-Pforte</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_ff308c21">
               <persName role="editor" xml:id="persName_50cd8600">Johann Mattheson</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_ideab5a2d1">
               <date isodate="1740" xml:id="date_7b7882d5">1740</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id8c2ddeea">
               <p xml:id="idm66">In 1739, Scheibe was asked to write his autobiography for <persName xml:id="idm67" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Mattheson</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm68" fontstyle="italic">Grundlage einer Ehren-Pforte</rend> which appeared the following year. The book included biographies of composers, musicians and Kapellmeisters of earlier times as well as contemporaries such as <persName xml:id="idm69" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christoph Graupner</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm70" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">George Frederic Handel</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm71" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Reinhard Keiser</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm72" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Jean Baptiste Lully</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm73" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName>. Apparently also <persName xml:id="idm74" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Sebastian Bach</persName> had been asked to send his biography though he never responded which indeed seems to have annoyed Mattheson (cf. <rend xml:id="idm75" fontstyle="italic">Kern melodischer Wißenschaft</rend>). At that time Scheibe had just been employed as Kapellmeister at the Court of the <persName xml:id="idm76" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Duke of Brandenburg-Kulmbach</persName> who was the brother-in-law to the Danish king. His autobiography is important as it tells us about his upbringing in Leipzig 1730–35, during which time he was tutored as well as providing lessons in composition and keyboard. Scheibe also recounts arriving in Hamburg in 1736, establishing both friends as well as enemies. It was through General <persName xml:id="idm77" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Ehrenfried Amthor</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm78" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Duke Ludwig Casimir von Isenburg</persName> that he was offered the position as Kapellmeister at the Court of Brandenburg-Kulmbach. The biography also explains the incident in his father's workshop which made Scheibe blind on the one eye – a detail which is also evident from the small portrait which still exists. Scheibe ends his autobiography briefly listing those theoretical and practical (that is, musical compositions) works he so far had finished: more than 150 ‘Kirchenstücke’ produced during the last six or seven years; more than 150 concertos for the flute and more than 30 for the violin; between 60 and 70 'sinfonien' as well as trios, solos, Italian and German cantatas; finally also large occasional serenatas ('starcke Serenaten') and 'Singgedichte', a couple of Passion oratorios, and an opera.</p>
            </history>
